/* HORIZONTAL CLEARANCE COMES FROM `.emb-bleed` ON THE ROOT, and this rule must not take it back.
 * Unlike the main menu, this screen has no absolutely-positioned chrome to hang `.emb-edge` on --
 * "Main Menu" and "View Collection" sit in a FLOW header -- so the only thing that can inset them
 * is the root. It was a `padding: var(--space-6)` shorthand until 2026-08-30, which put the two
 * top-right buttons 24dp from the edge, inside the 52dp cutout. Same bug the deck drawer's Close
 * had, arrived at a different way.
 *
 * LONGHANDS, NOT A SHORTHAND. A `padding:` here outranks nothing -- `.emb-bleed` and this rule are
 * both one class -- but the LATER of two equal-specificity declarations wins, and a shorthand
 * re-sets all four sides. Writing the vertical as longhands is what lets the horizontal survive
 * regardless of sheet order. The vertical came down 24 -> 8 each on 2026-08-31 (Sam); that is safe
 * to move because the roster solver MEASURES its viewport rather than deriving it -- the 48dp root
 * in CharacterRosterController.RowChrome's worked example is an illustration, not an input. */
.character-roster {
    flex-grow: 1;
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

.roster-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.roster-header-left {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.roster-header-actions {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.roster-header-actions .roster-button {
    margin-left: var(--space-2);
}

/* The one door into creation, top-left (NOR-278). CTA-gold so it reads as the primary thing to
 * do on an empty roster. Flush against the header's left edge (Sam, 2026-08-09) — it used to sit
 * right of a "Characters" / "N characters" heading that said nothing the roster below it didn't
 * already show, so the heading is gone and this no longer needs a margin to clear it. */
.new-character-button {
    min-width: 0;
    height: var(--touch-min);
    font-size: var(--type-label);
}

.roster-body {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
}

/* 8 all round. The vertical is the constrained axis -- this panel's height is the viewport
 * LayoutDeckStrip divides between rows, so every unit here is deck picture -- and the horizontal
 * came down to match on 2026-08-31 (Sam). (Authored when the panel was 411 tall, where 8 was a
 * real share of it. On the 1080 panel of #349 it is not; the value is left alone because it is
 * the sheet's spacing rhythm, but the row solve above it has NOT been re-measured.) */
.roster-panel {
    background-color: var(--surface-2);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    padding: var(--space-2);
}

.roster-panel--list {
    flex-grow: 7;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
}

.roster-panel--create {
    flex-grow: 3;
    min-width: 380px;
    max-width: 520px;
}

.character-roster--match-pick .roster-panel--list {
    margin-right: 0;
}

.character-roster--match-pick .roster-panel--create {
    display: none;
}

.roster-intro,
.create-help {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
}

.roster-intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.roster-scroll,
.roster-list {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 0;
}

.roster-empty {
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* No min-height: the plates decide how tall a row is now, and they are sized to the space the
 * strip actually has (CharacterRosterController.LayoutDeckStrip).
 *
 * A COLUMN, not a row (Sam, 2026-08-07): a name band, then the decks under it at the row's full
 * width. It was three side-by-side columns — name, strip, Delete — and the two text columns took
 * enough width off the strip to wrap a third deck onto a second line. */
.character-row {
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    border-left-width: 6px;
    border-left-color: var(--class-neutral);
    overflow: hidden;
}

.character-row--emberward { border-left-color: var(--class-emberward); }
.character-row--tidecaller { border-left-color: var(--class-tidecaller); }
.character-row--gravekeep { border-left-color: var(--class-gravekeep); }
.character-row--verdant { border-left-color: var(--class-verdant); }

/* The decks no character claims (collection-deckbuilder.md §5 O-4). It uses the character row's
 * shape so the plates inside it size the same way, and then reads as clearly NOT a character: the
 * spine is the destructive red rather than a class colour, and the identity block carries a
 * sentence where every other row carries a name. It is only ever drawn when there is at least one,
 * so a healthy collection never sees it. */
.character-row--orphan {
    border-left-color: var(--negative);
    background-color: var(--surface-0);
}

.character-name--orphan {
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    color: var(--ink-2);
}

/* Beside the count now, not under it — the identity block is a row. */
.orphan-note {
    font-size: var(--type-caption);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    flex-shrink: 1;
    white-space: normal;
}

/* The name band over the decks. It holds the name on the left and Delete on the right, and it is
 * the only thing in the row that is not deck art — which is the point: one line of height buys the
 * strip the row's whole width, where the old left-hand column charged 18% of it forever. */
/* EVERY unit in this band is one the deck picture does not get. RowChrome in
 * CharacterRosterController is the sum of this padding, the name's line, the strip's padding, the
 * plate's own chrome and the row's bottom margin — and it is subtracted from `viewport / rows`
 * before the plate is sized. It was tightened 112 -> 90 -> 70 on 2026-08-30 because the panel was
 * 411 tall then (#324) and 112 of chrome was 55% of a two-row screen.
 *
 * THE PRESSURE IS OFF AND THE TIGHTENING IS NOT WRONG (#349): the panel is 1080 tall now, so 70
 * is ~13% of a two-row screen rather than 55% and the solver is no longer pinned to its floor.
 * KEEP RowChrome IN STEP ANYWAY — it is a SUM OF THE VALUES IN THIS SHEET, which did not move,
 * so it is still correct and still breaks silently if this band is edited without it. */
.character-row-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-4);
}

/* The character's name, and nothing else (Sam, 2026-08-07). Anything else on it is a line the
 * player re-reads for information they had after the first row. A plain block, not a button:
 * opening a character means opening one of ITS DECKS, and there is no "just the character"
 * destination left for a click to go to. */
.character-identity {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

/* --type-h2, not --type-h1 (2026-08-30, #324): this line is pure RowChrome, and an h1 name cost
 * 6dp of deck picture on every row for a string the coloured spine already identifies. It is also
 * the row whose content was overflowing its band on the phone (#307). */
.character-name {
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink-1);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-right: var(--space-3);
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The pairing used to be spelled out under the name. The row's coloured spine carries the
 * primary class now, and the decks carry the rest (Sam, 2026-08-07). */

/* ---- Deck strip (Sam, 2026-08-07) ------------------------------------------------------
 * The deck switcher and the deck name used to live at the top of the deckbuilder's right-hand
 * panel. They live here now: a character row is mostly empty space, and a deck is a thing you
 * pick BEFORE you open an editor for it, not a tab inside one.
 *
 * A deck is drawn as ONE CARD and carries no name (Sam, 2026-08-07): the art the player chose
 * from inside it is the identity, the way a physical deck is known by its sleeve.
 */
/* NOWRAP (Sam, 2026-08-07). A deck that does not fit makes every plate on the row narrower; it
 * never drops to a second line. The row is full-width now precisely so this rarely bites, but the
 * responsive shrink is the behaviour we want when it does — LayoutDeckStrip divides the width by
 * the plate count with no wrap point to solve for. */
.deck-strip {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    /* Vertical halved 2026-08-30, horizontal 2026-08-31: the VERTICAL pair is RowChrome,
     * subtracted from every row's share of the viewport -- keep CharacterRosterController.RowChrome
     * in step with it, its docstring carries the sum. The horizontal is free. */
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2) var(--space-2) var(--space-2);
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* width, and the art's square side, are written per row by LayoutDeckStrip. The value here is
 * only what a plate looks like for the frame before that pass runs. */
.deck-plate {
    width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    align-items: center;
    /* Must match CharacterRosterController.PlateGutter -- every plate carries one, including the
     * last, so the width solve charges the strip for `plates` of them. 12 -> 8 on 2026-08-31. */
    margin: 0 var(--space-2) 0 0;
    /* Halved 2026-08-30. This padding is charged TWICE: horizontally it is
     * CharacterRosterController.PlateChrome, vertically it is part of RowChrome. Move it and both
     * constants move with it. */
    padding: var(--space-1);
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    background-color: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

.deck-image-scroll {
    max-height: 90%;
}

/* The art of whichever card fronts the deck, rendered SQUARE (Sam, 2026-08-07) — a deck is not
 * a card-shaped thumbnail here. scale-and-crop rather than stretch: card art is not square, and
 * letterboxing it reads as a bug where cropping reads as framing. The plate strip overwrites
 * these two values every layout pass; they are the picker's size, and the plate's first frame. */
.deck-plate-art {
    width: 112px;
    height: 112px;
    border-radius: var(--space-1);
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-and-crop;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* GOLD MEANS READY TO PLAY (Sam, 2026-08-07). It is the only thing on a plate that says so —
 * the "17 / 30" caption and the Ready/Draft chip before it are both gone. A deck short of 30, or
 * holding something illegal, simply has no gold edge.
 *
 * The default deck is NOT a border. It is the star on the plate's Functions button, because gold
 * now means something else and a second ring colour on the same element would be read as one
 * state, not two.
 *
 * The match pick is the OUTER ring on the plate, so a picked deck still shows its gold inner edge
 * — two concentric rings, two independent facts. */
.deck-plate-art--ready {
    border-width: 3px;
    border-color: var(--accent);
}

.deck-plate--selected {
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
    border-color: var(--mana);
}

/* An orphan's plate. The art is NOT dimmed — refuse the deck, never the picture (Sam's collection
 * ruling) — and it never takes the gold ready edge, because it fails L5 by construction. What
 * marks it is the dashed-looking muted ring: present enough to say "this one is different", quiet
 * enough that it does not compete with gold. */
.deck-plate--orphan .deck-plate-art {
    border-width: 3px;
    border-color: var(--class-neutral);
}

.deck-plate--orphan:hover .deck-plate-art {
    border-color: var(--negative);
}

/* The plate has no button (Sam, 2026-08-07). It is the picture, and the picture is the control:
 * one tap opens the functions, two open the deckbuilder. So the only affordance it can offer is
 * the hover itself. */
.deck-plate:hover .deck-plate-art {
    border-width: 3px;
    border-color: var(--ink-1);
}

.deck-plate--default .deck-plate-art:hover,
.deck-plate-art--ready:hover {
    border-color: var(--ink-1);
}

/* The default marker, over the art's top-left. A glyph rather than a word, and not a border —
 * gold is spoken for. */
.deck-plate-star {
    position: absolute;
    top: var(--space-1);
    left: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    color: var(--accent);
}

.deck-edit-hint {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.deck-plate--new .deck-plate-art {
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: var(--surface-3);
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
}

.deck-plate-plus {
    font-size: var(--type-display);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink-2);
}

.deck-plate--new:hover .deck-plate-art { border-color: var(--accent); }
.deck-plate--new:hover .deck-plate-plus { color: var(--accent); }

/* There is no deck-strip-hint any more. A character whose default deck was deleted rolls onto
 * another one silently (Sam, 2026-08-07) — an error message above somebody's decks, about a deck
 * they deleted on purpose, is not news they can act on. */

/* ---- Deck modal header: account name + class badges (NOR-404) --------------------------------
 * The header row sits above the deck title. Account name is on the left; class badges pin to
 * the right. Hidden on orphan dialogs (no character, so no classes to show). */
.deck-modal-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.deck-modal-account {
    font-size: var(--type-caption);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    flex-shrink: 1;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.deck-modal-classes {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-left: var(--space-3);
}

.deck-modal-class-badge {
    font-size: var(--type-caption);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--surface-0);
    padding: 2px var(--space-2);
    border-radius: 10px;
    margin-left: var(--space-1);
    background-color: var(--class-neutral);
}

.deck-modal-class-badge--emberward  { background-color: var(--class-emberward); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--tidecaller { background-color: var(--class-tidecaller); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--gravekeep  { background-color: var(--class-gravekeep); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--verdant    { background-color: var(--class-verdant); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--tempest    { background-color: var(--class-tempest); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--clockwright { background-color: var(--class-clockwright); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--stonewarden { background-color: var(--class-stonewarden); }
.deck-modal-class-badge--nightveil  { background-color: var(--class-nightveil); }

/* ---- Deck modal panes ---- */
.deck-pane {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.deck-action {
    align-self: stretch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.deck-back-grid {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.deck-back-option {
    width: 128px;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 var(--space-3) var(--space-3) 0;
    padding: var(--space-2);
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: var(--surface-3);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

.deck-back-option--on {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
}

/* Pickers label their options; deck plates on the roster do not. A card back has no other name,
 * and the deck-image grid identifies its cards by tooltip because the art is the point. */
.deck-back-option .deck-plate-name {
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--type-caption);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* ---- Character menu (Sam, 2026-08-07) --------------------------------------------------------
 * The row's Delete button is gone. It was a full-height spine down the right edge — permanent
 * width, on the one action nobody wants within a stray tap of the deck they meant to open. The
 * name opens it now.
 *
 * A popover, not a modal: the card is positioned under the name by
 * CharacterRosterController.OpenCharacterMenu, and the scrim behind it is transparent rather than
 * dimming, because this is a menu about one row and darkening the screen says otherwise. The
 * scrim still fills the panel — it is what makes "click anywhere else" close it, and what stops a
 * click landing on a deck plate underneath. */
.character-menu {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

.character-menu--hidden {
    display: none;
}

.character-menu-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
}

/* left/top are written per open. Width is fixed so the anchor pass has something to clamp
 * against before the card has ever been laid out. */
.character-menu-card {
    position: absolute;
    width: 260px;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    background-color: var(--surface-2);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--surface-3);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
}

.character-menu-title {
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The name is the control. Nothing else in the row is text you can click, so it says so on
 * hover rather than wearing a button's chrome permanently. The orphan row's heading is a
 * sentence, not a character, and opens nothing — so it must not offer the tell. */
.character-name:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
}

.character-name--orphan:hover {
    color: var(--ink-2);
}

/* Find Game's Continue bar. Match pick used to be a whole second row layout — text buttons per
 * deck, its own draft/empty states — which meant the same screen looked different depending on
 * which door you came through. It draws the ordinary plates now (Sam, 2026-08-07); this bar is
 * the only thing the match door adds. */
.match-bar {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
}


.create-title,
.delete-title {
    font-size: var(--type-h1);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.create-help {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
}

.create-help--starter {
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

.roster-field {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

.roster-field .unity-base-field__label {
    min-width: 120px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--type-body);
}

.roster-field .unity-base-text-field__input,
.roster-field .unity-base-popup-field__input {
    min-height: var(--touch-min);
    background-color: var(--surface-0);
    border-color: var(--surface-3);
    border-radius: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    padding: var(--space-2);
}

.create-order-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

.power-hint {
    flex-grow: 1;
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    color: var(--accent);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.create-error,
.roster-status {
    min-height: 24px;
    font-size: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--negative);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

/* THE STATUS LINE IS NOT LAID OUT UNTIL IT HAS SOMETHING TO SAY. It is a sibling of the roster
 * ScrollView, so its 24dp floor plus its 8dp top margin came straight off the VIEWPORT the deck
 * plates are solved against -- 32 of ~283dp, permanently, for a Label that is empty on every
 * screen a player ever sees. `visibility: hidden` would not do it; only display: none takes an
 * element out of the layout. CharacterRosterController.SetStatus owns the modifier: a message
 * turns it on, an empty one turns it back off. */
.roster-status {
    display: none;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

.roster-status--on {
    display: flex;
}

.roster-status--error {
    color: var(--negative);
}

/* THIS CLASS ALONE DOES NOT MAKE A VISIBLE BUTTON. It sets geometry and type and deliberately
 * leaves `color` and `background-color` to a modifier — --cta, --ghost, --danger. A button given
 * `roster-button` and no modifier does not fall back to anything of ours: it renders as Unity's
 * DEFAULT runtime button, flat light grey with a mid-grey label, and nothing warns. That is what
 * shipped on the whole hero picker (#311/#314/#325) — the screen was read as "missing its
 * stylesheet" when every class it uses was in fact defined right here.
 * Add a modifier, or set the two properties yourself, whenever you add this class. */
.roster-button {
    min-width: 148px;
    height: 48px;
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    padding-right: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
}

.roster-button--small {
    min-width: 128px;
    height: var(--touch-min);
    font-size: var(--type-label);
}

.roster-button--cta {
    color: var(--surface-0);
    background-color: var(--accent);
}

.roster-button--ghost {
    color: var(--ink-1);
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
}

.roster-button--danger {
    color: var(--ink-1);
    background-color: var(--negative);
}

.create-button {
    align-self: stretch;
}

.delete-modal {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.72);
}

.delete-modal--hidden {
    display: none;
}

/* Sized for the menu pane (a stack of buttons) — the common case for opening this dialog.
 * .delete-dialog--wide (below) overrides this for the two panes that actually need the room:
 * a grid of card art wants width the button list would only stretch to fill (Sam, 2026-08-09).
 * Same override pattern as .character-create-dialog just below. */
.delete-dialog {
    width: 50%;
    max-width: 60%;
    height: 80%;
    padding: var(--space-6);
    background-color: var(--surface-2);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--surface-3);
}

.delete-dialog--wide {
    width: 80%;
    max-width: 88%;
}

.delete-message {
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
}

.delete-actions {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    margin-top: auto;
}

.delete-actions .roster-button {
    margin-left: var(--space-3);
}

/* ---- Character creation ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* THE PANEL IS 2400 x 1080 UNITS AT 1.000x, ONE UNIT PER DEVICE PIXEL — NOT 914 x 411dp.
 * `EmberfallPanelSettings.m_ScaleMode` went 1 -> 2 (ConstantPhysicalSize -> ScaleWithScreenSize)
 * in 02048077 on 2026-09-01, against a 1920x1080 reference matched on HEIGHT. referenceDpi is
 * still 160 in the asset and is now INERT — ScaleWithScreenSize does not read it — so a comment
 * anywhere in this project that reasons from "411dp" or "one authored px is one Android dp" is
 * describing a panel that no longer exists. Measured on the live panel 2026-09-02:
 *
 *     panel  2400.0 x 1080.0    scale=1.000x
 *     .emb-root classes: emb-root character-create-screen emb-bleed emb-sw-expanded
 *
 * That trailing `emb-sw-expanded` is why the screen classes are GONE (#349, 2026-09-03). The
 * panel's short axis is now a constant 1080 on every device, the classes keyed on that axis
 * against 600/840, so `expanded` was the only one that could ever be applied. Its values are
 * folded into the base blocks and the other two branches are deleted. A measurement taken today
 * prints `emb-root character-create-screen emb-bleed` and nothing more.
 *
 * WHAT THAT DID TO THIS SCREEN. Everything authored against 411 is now ~2.6x too small: the two
 * picker columns are capped at 420 units and sat in the middle of a 2296-unit row, leaving 1456
 * units of nothing, and the plates were 84 units tall on a 1080-unit screen. The sizes below are
 * re-authored for the real panel and are deliberately EXPLICIT PIXELS rather than tokens, which
 * is the same call collection.uss makes for the one thing it has to get right at this scale
 * (`--card-tile-width: 274; --card-tile-height: 384` — a card tile is 36% of the screen's height).
 * The spacing and type tokens are still 411-era values — #349 retired the screen-class run they
 * stepped in, but not the values themselves — and are used below only where something genuinely
 * is small (a gutter, a caption).
 *
 * NEVER WRITE A TOKEN GLOB AS `--space-<star>/--type-<star>` IN A USS COMMENT. That sequence
 * contains the comment terminator, so it ends the comment early, the prose after it is parsed as
 * declarations, and UI Toolkit DROPS THE ENTIRE STYLESHEET with no error. Measured 2026-09-02: the
 * picker came back as two full-width stacked columns with 0-height portraits and 24-unit text —
 * every rule in this 900-line file gone, including ones nobody had touched in weeks.
 *
 * THIS SCREEN AUTHORS ITS OWN VERTICAL GUTTER, and has to. `.emb-bleed` is its only inset and
 * `.emb-bleed` is `0 var(--edge-safe)` since 568d6b33 -- the cutout is on a SIDE, so the class
 * deliberately no longer imposes a top or bottom (see the token's note in theme.uss). Nothing
 * else in the picker column has a top margin, so without these two the Cancel button in
 * `.create-picker-header` sits at y=0 and clips its own top corners. LONGHANDS, not a `padding`
 * shorthand: the shorthand would re-set the horizontal padding `.emb-bleed` is here to supply. */
.character-create-screen {
    flex-grow: 1;
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-4);
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    justify-content: center;
}

#create-picker-view {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 0;
}

.create-picker-header {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.create-picker-header-spacer {
    width: 148px;
}

.create-picker-heading {
    flex-grow: 1;
    align-items: center;
}

/* --type-h1 is 36 at the expanded screen class, which is a caption on a 1080-unit panel, not a
 * screen title. Sized here rather than by moving the token because .delete-title is worn by the
 * detail and confirm views and by the roster's own dialogs. */
.create-picker-heading #create-pass-title {
    font-size: 52px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
}

/* The pairing explanation (#312). Sized under the title rather than beside the tokens: every unit
 * this line takes comes straight off .hero-choice's height, and the header is already competing
 * with eight plates. white-space: normal so a long second-pass sentence wraps inside the heading
 * column rather than pushing Cancel off the row. */
.create-pass-help {
    font-size: 30px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
}

#create-detail-view,
#create-confirm-view {
    align-self: center;
    width: 720px;
    max-width: 100%;
}

#create-pass-title {
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* Fixed columns keep all eight choices predictable on a short landscape phone. The picker has
 * no wrapping, geometry callbacks, inline dimensions, portraits, badges, or nested hit targets. */
/* align-items: STRETCH is what makes the columns full height, and it is the declaration the dead
 * space was hiding behind. This is a ROW, so align-items is its CROSS axis: at `center` each
 * column sized to its four buttons and was centred, which meant the column's own
 * `justify-content` had no surplus to distribute and could never have had any. The two bands of
 * nothing above and below the list were that, not an absence of sizing rules on the buttons. */
.hero-choice-list {
    flex-grow: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: stretch;
    justify-content: space-evenly;
}

/* space-evenly, not center (Sam, 2026-08-30). Four content-sized buttons centred in a full-height
 * column left the surplus as one band above and a bigger one below -- dead space at the top and
 * bottom of the screen rather than air between the things you are choosing between. Evenly spends
 * it as equal gaps, including the outer two, so the column reads as a list rather than a clump.
 *
 * THE CAP WAS 420 AND THE ROW IS 2296, so the two columns used 840 units and left 1456 of nothing
 * either side — the single loudest symptom of the scale change at the top of this section. 1150
 * lets the pair fill the row and still stops a wide desktop stretching a plate into a banner;
 * `.db-collection` makes the same call by filling to a 24-unit inset rather than centring. */
/* flex-basis: 0 IS WHAT MAKES THE TWO COLUMNS THE SAME WIDTH. `flex-grow: 1` alone leaves the
 * basis at `auto`, so each column starts from its own INTRINSIC width — the widest plate in it —
 * and grows from there. The plates differ only in their hero-power text, so Tempest's "Chain Spark
 * · Deal 1 damage twice to random enemies." made its column start wider and finish wider:
 * measured 1114 against 1150, a 36-unit step between two things meant to be a matched pair. */
.hero-choice-column {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    max-width: 1150px;
    margin-left: var(--space-2);
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
    justify-content: space-evenly;
}

/* THE PLATE (#312). Portrait, class name, hero power line — a flex ROW, laid out entirely by the
 * declarations below. Nothing in the picker measures anything; see BuildHeroChoice's note on why
 * that is the load-bearing property of this design and not an implementation detail.
 *
 * The colours are HERE, not on a --ghost modifier, because these buttons carry `roster-button`
 * and nothing else — and `roster-button` sets no colour of its own (see the note on it above).
 * Without these two properties the eight hero choices rendered as Unity's default grey button:
 * #311, #314 and #315 are all that one omission, and #325 called it a missing stylesheet.
 *
 * The class-coloured spine stays. It was the only thing distinguishing one choice from another
 * before the art landed; it is now the thing that still says which class this is at a glance when
 * the portrait is a dark silhouette, and it is the same language a character row speaks. */
/* flex-grow: 1 with flex-basis: 0 -- the buttons take an equal share of the column's height rather
 * than sizing to their label, so the surplus goes INTO the targets. Sam, 2026-08-30: bigger is
 * better but the space is what it is, so this spends the surplus rather than chasing a size.
 *
 * THE max-height IS WHAT LEAVES ANY GAPS AT ALL. flex-grow and the column's space-evenly compete
 * for the same surplus and flex-grow resolves first: uncapped, four plates eat the column's 963
 * units whole, land at ~240 each and touch edge to edge, and space-evenly distributes nothing.
 *
 * 84 -> 212, WHICH IS THE SCALE CORRECTION AND NOT A TASTE CHANGE. 72 was authored when the
 * column was ~367 units tall; 84 was that number nudged for the art. The column is 963 units, so
 * both were about a fifth of the size the screen was actually offering — measured, four 84s and
 * five 126-unit bands of nothing. 4 x 212 = 848 of 963, leaving 115 for space-evenly to spend as
 * five 23-unit gaps.
 *
 * 212 - 2x--space-1 padding = a 204-unit square portrait, which is what .hero-choice-portrait is
 * sized to. MOVE ONE AND MOVE THE OTHER — the portrait's height comes from align-self: stretch,
 * so only its width is written and only this rule decides whether it is square.
 *
 * min-height stays as the floor for a screen with no surplus; margin-bottom is gone because
 * space-evenly owns the gaps now and the two would compound. */
.hero-choice {
    align-self: stretch;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    max-height: 212px;
    min-height: var(--touch-min);
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    /* SHORTHAND ON PURPOSE, and it is the opposite call from .character-create-screen's. That rule
     * uses longhands so it cannot clobber the horizontal padding .emb-bleed supplies; this one is
     * here precisely TO clobber `.roster-button`'s 16dp left/right, which would otherwise push the
     * portrait 16dp off the spine and cost the text the same again on the other side. */
    padding: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    background-color: var(--surface-2);
    /* 6 -> 14 and 4 -> 12 for the same reason every other number in this block moved: `.unity-button`
     * supplies a 4-unit radius that was a soft corner on an 84-unit plate and is a hard one on a
     * 212-unit plate, and a 6-unit spine beside a 204-unit portrait is a hairline. */
    border-left-width: 14px;
    border-left-color: var(--class-neutral);
    border-radius: 12px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.hero-choice:hover {
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
}

/* THE FULL-PLATE ART WASH WAS TRIED AND CUT, and the reason is worth keeping because it is the
 * obvious thing to reach for again. Each plate carried its own hero art full-bleed at 0.22 under a
 * flat scrim — the .craft-illustration/.craft-illustration-scrim shape. Rendered at 2400x1080 on
 * 2026-09-02 it failed on two counts a mock-up would not show:
 *
 *   1. A 1124x212 plate is a 5:1 letterbox, so the wash is a CROP THROUGH THE MIDDLE of a square
 *      full-body portrait — a torso and a background, never a face. It is noise, not subject.
 *   2. The eight source images have wildly different values. Stonewarden's bright sky washed to a
 *      pale grey block with a hard crop edge; Gravekeep's and Verdant's dark art vanished into
 *      --surface-2 entirely. Eight plates meant to read as one set read as eight different
 *      treatments, and the bright ones looked like a rendering fault rather than a design.
 *
 * A flat plate plus one well-cropped square portrait is what the collection wall does with far
 * more art on screen, and it is the discipline this screen was missing. If a ground is wanted
 * later it should be ONE authored backdrop shared by all eight, or per-class art cropped for a
 * 5:1 band on purpose — not the square portrait stretched into a letterbox. */

/* align-self: stretch rather than a height, so the portrait is always exactly the plate's content
 * height and stays square at the 212 cap without either number being written twice. flex-shrink: 0
 * because a narrow column must eat into the TEXT, never into the likeness. */
.hero-choice-portrait {
    align-self: stretch;
    width: 204px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-and-crop;
    margin-right: var(--space-6);
}

.hero-choice-body {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* EXPLICIT, NOT --type-h2. The token is 26 — set when 26 authored units were 26dp of a 411-unit
 * panel, i.e. 6% of its height. (It was the `expanded` step of a screen-class run; #349 deleted
 * the run and folded that step into the base, so 26 is simply the value now.) The same 26 is 2.4% of 1080 and
 * reads as a caption beside a 204-unit portrait. 56 restores the proportion the token was chosen
 * for. Same reasoning for the power line below. */
.hero-choice-name {
    font-size: 56px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

/* white-space: normal so the two long powers (Chain Spark, Backstab) wrap to a second line rather
 * than being cut mid-word. There is room for it now: 56 + 2 x ~40 = 136 of the 204 the plate has,
 * where at the old size a two-line power measured 45 against 38 of remaining space and clipped. */
.hero-choice-power {
    font-size: 32px;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
}

.hero-choice-power--muted {
    opacity: 0.5;
}

/* The hero power's socket ring, on the right of the plate where there was dead space (#312).
 *
 * ITS SIZE IS DERIVED FROM THE SPRITE, NOT PICKED, AND THE DERIVATION IS THE MAINTENANCE BURDEN.
 * HeroPower_IronEmber.png is 1424x1424 with a 730x747 aperture, so the hole is 0.5126 of the file.
 * Drawn at 200 the hole is ~103 across, and the glyph inside is sized to 92 so a little metal
 * always overlaps it rather than the two meeting on a seam.
 *
 * BOTH NUMBERS MOVE WHEN THE SPRITE IS RE-CUT, and nothing warns. This ring has already been
 * re-exported twice: the first install was ap/canvas 0.5748, where 200 would have given a 115 hole
 * and left the 92 glyph swimming in it. `align_socket_rings.py` prints the ap/canvas figure on
 * every run — read it off the run that produced the file you are installing, and update the two
 * numbers below in the same commit.
 *
 * The ring is a plain background-image, not a 9-slice: it is round, and slicing a circle stretches
 * its middle into a capsule. Fixed size and scale-to-fit are the only correct treatment. */
.hero-choice-ring {
    width: 200px;
    height: 200px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    align-self: center;
    margin-left: var(--space-4);
    background-image: url("/Assets/Art/UI/Frames/HeroPower_IronEmber.png");
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-to-fit;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* scale-to-fit, NOT scale-and-crop: the hero-power arts are square and full-bleed, and cropping
 * one into the ring's circle would cut the glyph rather than frame it. */
.hero-choice-glyph {
    width: 92px;
    height: 92px;
    border-radius: 46px;
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-to-fit;
}

/* 1 or 2 — which slot this class filled. It replaces the "1 · " prefix the label used to carry,
 * which put the ordinal where the eye reads the class NAME. */
.hero-choice-badge {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 72px;
    height: 72px;
    border-radius: 36px;
    margin-left: var(--space-4);
    margin-right: var(--space-3);
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--surface-0);
    font-size: 40px;
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

/* One per CardClass, keyed on the ENUM MEMBER LOWERCASED — `tempest`, `stonewarden`, never
 * `storm` or `warden`. CharacterCreateController.BuildHeroChoice mints the token; there is no
 * name map on either side, so a class added to the enum needs a row here or its spine is grey. */
.hero-choice--emberward   { border-left-color: var(--class-emberward); }
.hero-choice--tidecaller  { border-left-color: var(--class-tidecaller); }
.hero-choice--gravekeep   { border-left-color: var(--class-gravekeep); }
.hero-choice--verdant     { border-left-color: var(--class-verdant); }
.hero-choice--tempest     { border-left-color: var(--class-tempest); }
.hero-choice--clockwright { border-left-color: var(--class-clockwright); }
.hero-choice--stonewarden { border-left-color: var(--class-stonewarden); }
.hero-choice--nightveil   { border-left-color: var(--class-nightveil); }

/* Border-width on all four sides would replace the spine, so the selected state is carried by the
 * fill and the text instead — the spine keeps saying which class this is.
 *
 * THE NAME IS TINTED BY ITS OWN RULE, not by inheritance. `color` on the plate reaches the Button's
 * own text, and since #312 the Button's text is empty — every word on the plate is a child Label
 * with a `color` of its own, and an explicit declaration beats an inherited one. The plate's
 * `color` is left in place for the same reason `-unity-text-align` is: it costs nothing and it is
 * what a plain roster-button still expects. */
.hero-choice--selected {
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--accent);
}

.hero-choice--selected .hero-choice-name {
    color: var(--accent);
}
/* ---- Detail overlay ---- */
.hero-detail-head {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
}

.hero-detail-portrait {
    width: 96px;
    height: 96px;
    border-radius: var(--space-1);
    background-color: var(--surface-3);
    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-and-crop;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-right: var(--space-4);
}

.hero-detail-headings {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
}

.hero-detail-pass {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: normal;
}

/* Traits/playstyle bullets, filled from hero-detail.json (NOR-281). */
.hero-detail-bullets {
    min-height: 72px;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
}

.hero-detail-bullet {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
}

/* Filled from the class's hero power card since NOR-291. The minimum height is now a floor for a
 * one-line power rather than a placeholder — it keeps the overlay the same height whether the
 * generated description wraps to one line or two. */
.hero-power-block {
    min-height: 64px;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-radius: var(--space-2);
    background-color: var(--surface-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
}

.hero-power-caption {
    font-size: var(--type-caption);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
}

.hero-power-name {
    font-size: var(--type-h2);
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    color: var(--ink-1);
}

.hero-power-desc {
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    white-space: normal;
}

.unity-button {
    border-radius: 4px;
}
